  ['Shots, scenes and sequences', 12, "<p>Films are built from small units:</p><ul><li><strong>Shot:</strong> one continuous piece of footage from the moment the camera starts until it stops.</li><li><strong>Scene:</strong> a series of shots in one place and time.</li><li><strong>Sequence:</strong> a group of scenes forming a larger part of the story.</li></ul><p><strong>Show, don’t tell:</strong> use pictures and actions instead of explaining in words.</p>"],
  ['How images create meaning', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Camera angle:</strong> a low angle can make a character look powerful; a high angle can make them look small.</li><li><strong>Movement:</strong> a slow push-in builds tension; handheld feels urgent and real.</li><li><strong>Colour and light:</strong> warm colours can suggest comfort or danger; hard shadows suggest conflict.</li><li><strong>Editing:</strong> putting two shots together creates a third idea (a person looking, then a plate of food, tells us they are hungry).</li><li><strong>Sound and music:</strong> guide how we feel; silence can be powerful.</li></ul>"],
  ['Genre, theme and point of view', 14, "<p><strong>Genre</strong> is a group of films that share conventions (comedy, drama, thriller, horror, romance, documentary). Audiences expect certain things from a genre, and good filmmakers use or surprise those expectations.</p><p><strong>Theme</strong> is the idea underneath the story (such as justice, belonging or sacrifice). <strong>Point of view</strong> is whose eyes we see the story through.</p><p><strong>Try this:</strong> watch a short scene twice. First note the story; then turn off the sound and note what the pictures alone tell you.</p>"],
