 'lessons' => [
  ['The look of the film', 14, "<p>The <strong>production designer</strong> leads the look of the sets and locations. The <strong>costume designer</strong> and <strong>make-up artist</strong> work with the director and cinematographer to create characters.</p><ul><li>Start with the script: <strong>where</strong> and <strong>when</strong> does the story happen, and <strong>who</strong> are these people?</li><li>Build a <strong>mood board</strong> of photos, colours and textures.</li><li>Choose a limited <strong>colour palette</strong> — it makes a film look designed even on a small budget.</li></ul>"],
  ['Locations, set dressing and props', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Real locations</strong> are cheaper than building sets. Choose a place that already matches the story and then adjust it.</li><li><strong>Set dressing:</strong> furniture, posters, plants, things on the walls. Remove anything modern or distracting that does not belong.</li><li><strong>Props:</strong> objects the actors handle. Make a prop list from the breakdown and buy, borrow or make them.</li><li>Photograph everything before the shoot and after each scene so you can restore the set.</li><li>Watch for <strong>visible brands and logos</strong>; cover or avoid them unless you have permission.</li></ul>"],
  ['Costume and make-up', 16, "<ul><li>Costume tells us who a person is: their job, income, mood and culture. Use colour to separate characters.</li><li>Check that clothes <strong>move well</strong>, are comfortable and suit the climate.</li><li>Have <strong>duplicates</strong> of important costumes for messy scenes.</li><li>Make-up should suit the camera and lighting. Do not use products that irritate the skin; ask about allergies.</li><li>Keep photos and notes for <strong>continuity</strong>: hair, buttons, jewellery, wounds, dirt.</li></ul>"],
  ['Budget, safety and continuity', 14, "<ul><li><strong>Borrow, reuse and rent</strong> before buying; plan to return things in the condition you found them.</li><li>Prioritise what the camera will <strong>actually see</strong>; do not spend on things that will be out of focus or out of frame.</li><li><strong>Safety:</strong> use real fire, weapons or chemicals only under qualified supervision; fake or safe alternatives are better.</li><li>Keep a <strong>continuity book</strong> with photos for each scene so objects, costumes and make-up match from day to day.</li></ul>"],
