 'slug' => 'production-management-and-set-safety', 'title' => 'Assistant Directing, Production Management and Set Safety', 'level' => 'Intermediate', 'category' => 'Producing & production management', 'duration' => '3 hours',
 'summary' => 'Keep a set organised, on time and safe: the assistant director’s role, call sheets, communication and risk management.',
 'description' => 'The assistant director (AD) runs the set so the director can direct. This course teaches schedules, set communication, health and safety, and how to look after cast and crew.',
 'outcomes' => "Explain the assistant director’s duties\nRun a shooting day from call time to wrap\nCommunicate using standard set calls\nCarry out a basic risk assessment\nHandle emergencies and welfare calmly",
 'lessons' => [
  ['What the assistant director does', 14, "<p>The <strong>1st AD</strong> manages the schedule and the set, calls “action” on the director’s cue and makes sure the day stays on plan. <strong>2nd ADs</strong> manage cast and call sheets; <strong>3rd ADs</strong> and <strong>runners</strong> help with extras, messages and logistics.</p><ul><li>Know the schedule and the call sheet by heart.</li><li>Keep everyone informed of what is next.</li><li>Protect the director’s time: handle problems before they reach them.</li></ul>"],
  ['Running the day', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Call time:</strong> cast and crew arrive; give a short safety briefing.</li><li><strong>Order:</strong> follow the schedule but adapt if light, weather or performances change.</li><li><strong>Set calls:</strong> “Quiet please, rolling!”, “Sound speed!”, “Camera speed!”, “Action!”, “Cut!” and “Reset!” keep everyone synchronised.</li><li>Do regular <strong>time checks</strong> and tell the director when you are behind.</li><li><strong>Wrap:</strong> check equipment, hand back locations clean, collect releases and send tomorrow’s call sheet.</li></ul>"],
  ['Health and safety', 18, "<ul><li>Do a <strong>risk assessment</strong> for each location: what could go wrong (traffic, water, heat, electricity, heights, crowds), how likely it is and what you will do about it.</li><li>Keep cables taped and walkways clear; secure stands and lights with sandbags.</li><li>Provide <strong>first aid</strong>, clean drinking water and shade, and know the nearest hospital.</li><li>Never allow unsafe stunts or unqualified people to do dangerous tasks.</li><li>Special care when working with <strong>children</strong>: guardian consent, limited hours, a guardian present and breaks.</li></ul>"],
  ['People, welfare and emergencies', 12, "<ul><li>Treat everyone with respect; harassment and bullying are never acceptable. Make sure there is someone cast and crew can report concerns to.</li><li>Plan <strong>meal breaks</strong> and rest. Tired crews make mistakes.</li><li>In an emergency: <strong>stop work, make the area safe, call for medical help and inform the producer</strong>. Write a short incident report afterwards.</li><li>Keep a calm voice; people follow how you behave.</li></ul>"],
