 'slug' => 'colour-and-post-production', 'title' => 'Colour Grading and Post-Production Finishing', 'level' => 'Advanced', 'category' => 'Editing & post-production', 'duration' => '4 hours',
 'summary' => 'Finish a film professionally: colour workflow, scopes, grading, audio post, quality control and deliverables.',
 'description' => 'For editors and filmmakers who want a polished final film. Learn a safe colour workflow, how to read scopes, how to finish sound and how to prepare deliverables.',
 'outcomes' => "Describe a colour workflow from log footage to final grade\nRead the waveform, histogram and vectorscope\nBalance shots and create a consistent look\nPlan ADR, sound effects and a final mix\nPrepare, check and deliver final files",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Colour workflow', 18, "<ol><li><strong>Lock the picture</strong> before final colour.</li><li><strong>Normalise:</strong> bring footage to a common starting point; if you shot in log, apply a conversion (a LUT or colour-managed transform).</li><li><strong>Primary correction:</strong> set exposure, contrast, white balance and saturation shot by shot.</li><li><strong>Match shots</strong> within each scene.</li><li><strong>Secondary correction:</strong> adjust specific colours or areas (skin, sky).</li><li><strong>Creative look:</strong> a consistent style across the film.</li></ol><p>Work in a dim room with a calibrated monitor where possible.</p>"],
  ['Scopes: trusting numbers, not just eyes', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Histogram:</strong> shows how many pixels are dark, mid or bright. Avoid crushing blacks or clipping whites.</li><li><strong>Waveform:</strong> shows brightness across the frame; skin tones usually sit in the middle range.</li><li><strong>Vectorscope:</strong> shows colour (hue and saturation); skin tones sit along the “skin tone line”.</li></ul><p>Screens differ, so scopes give a reliable reference. Check your grade on a phone and a TV as well.</p>"],
  ['Audio post: dialogue, ADR and mix', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Dialogue edit:</strong> clean and smooth all tracks; use room tone to fill gaps.</li><li><strong>ADR (additional dialogue recording):</strong> actors re-record lines in a studio to replace unusable production sound; match the performance and room sound.</li><li><strong>Foley and effects:</strong> add footsteps, cloth, props and ambient sound to make the world feel real.</li><li><strong>Final mix:</strong> balance dialogue, music and effects; check loudness so the film is comfortable to hear and does not distort.</li></ul>"],
  ['Quality control and deliverables', 14, "<ul><li><strong>Watch the entire film</strong> carefully on the correct equipment and check for glitches, wrong subtitles, flashing, audio drop-outs and spelling mistakes.</li><li>Prepare a <strong>master file</strong> in high quality, plus smaller versions for web and festivals.</li><li><strong>Subtitles:</strong> prepare an English subtitle file and any other languages needed, with accurate timing.</li><li><strong>Documents:</strong> credits list, music cue sheet, copies of licences and releases, a synopsis and stills.</li><li>Archive the project, footage and masters in two places.</li></ul>"],
 ],
