 'slug' => 'distribution-festivals-and-marketing', 'title' => 'Distribution, Festivals and Marketing', 'level' => 'Advanced', 'category' => 'Film business & distribution', 'duration' => '3.5 hours',
 'summary' => 'Get your film seen: distribution routes, festival strategy, marketing materials and building an audience.',
 'description' => 'Making a film is half the job. This course covers how films reach audiences — festivals, streaming, cinemas and community screenings — and how to plan marketing from the beginning.',
 'outcomes' => "Compare distribution routes and choose one for your film\nPlan a festival strategy and manage submissions\nCreate a poster, trailer and press kit\nPlan community and online audience building\nPrepare the deliverables distributors ask for",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Distribution routes', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Festivals:</strong> build prestige, reviews and industry contacts.</li><li><strong>Streaming and broadcast platforms:</strong> reach large audiences; usually through a sales agent or by application.</li><li><strong>Cinema release:</strong> costly, usually for features with a distributor.</li><li><strong>Self-distribution:</strong> upload to YouTube or Vimeo, sell tickets for screenings or direct downloads.</li><li><strong>Community and educational screenings:</strong> reach audiences who connect to the subject; add discussion.</li></ul><p>Decide who the film is for first; the best route follows from that audience.</p>"],
  ['Festival strategy', 18, "<ul><li>Many festivals prefer <strong>premieres</strong> (first public showing), so do not release online before submitting.</li><li>Research festivals: what do they programme, who attends, when are the deadlines and fees?</li><li>Apply to a <strong>mix</strong> of top, regional and niche festivals; ask about <strong>fee waivers</strong> and early-bird rates.</li><li>Submit through platforms such as FilmFreeway or the festival’s own portal; read the rules about length, language and completion date.</li><li>Prepare <strong>subtitles</strong>, a screener link and a short bio, synopsis and stills.</li><li>Keep a spreadsheet of submissions, deadlines and results.</li></ul>"],
  ['Marketing materials and audience', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Poster</strong> with strong image, title, key credits and festival laurels.</li><li><strong>Trailer</strong> of 1–2 minutes: hook early, show the world and the stakes, end with the title; do not spoil the ending.</li><li><strong>Press kit / EPK:</strong> synopsis, director’s statement, bios, stills, trailer link and contact details.</li><li><strong>Social media and community:</strong> share behind-the-scenes content from the start, tag cast and crew and invite local partners.</li><li>Keep a <strong>mailing list</strong> of people who want to hear about screenings.</li></ul>"],
  ['Deliverables and next steps', 14, "<ul><li>Distributors and festivals commonly request: a high-quality master or screening file, subtitles, a music cue sheet, copies of music and archive licences, credits, a dialogue list and a copyright statement.</li><li>Read every contract carefully, and get legal advice before signing a distribution deal. Check the length of the licence, territories, fees and who owns what.</li><li>Keep track of audience feedback and reviews — they help your next film.</li></ul>"],
