[
 'slug' => 'film-business-rights-and-ethics', 'title' => 'Film Business, Rights and Ethics', 'level' => 'Advanced', 'category' => 'Film business & distribution', 'duration' => '3.5 hours',
 'summary' => 'Protect your work and respect others: copyright basics, agreements, music licensing, funding and pitching, and ethical practice.',
 'description' => 'A film is a business and a responsibility. This course explains the basic ideas behind rights, agreements and funding, and how to work ethically. It is general education, not legal advice — always consult a qualified lawyer for your situation.',
 'outcomes' => "Explain copyright ownership and chain of title in simple terms\nIdentify the agreements a production needs\nUnderstand music licensing basics\nPrepare a funding pitch and budget summary\nApply ethical principles on set and in storytelling",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Copyright and chain of title', 14, "<p><strong>Copyright</strong> protects original creative work such as scripts, music and films. The owner decides who may copy, change or show it. Rules differ from country to country, so check the law where you work.</p><ul><li>Who owns the script? The writer — unless an agreement transfers the rights.</li><li><strong>Chain of title</strong> is the paper trail that proves you hold the rights to make and sell the film (from the writer or original source to you).</li><li>Never use someone else’s story, music, photos or clips without permission or a valid licence.</li></ul>"],
  ['Agreements every production needs', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Crew and cast agreements:</strong> pay, hours, credit, who owns the work, safety and behaviour.</li><li><strong>Location agreements:</strong> permission to film at a place, dates, conditions and damage.</li><li><strong>Releases:</strong> permission to use someone’s image and voice.</li><li><strong>Co-production or investor agreements:</strong> who contributes what and how profits and credit are shared.</li><li>Put agreements in <strong>writing</strong>, in plain language that everyone understands, and sign before the work starts.</li></ul>"],
  ['Music and licensing', 14, "<p>Using music in a film often needs <strong>two permissions</strong>: for the composition (sync licence) and for the specific recording (master licence). Other options are:</p><ul><li><strong>Commission</strong> original music from a composer under a written agreement.</li><li>Use <strong>royalty-free or openly licensed</strong> music — read the licence for how you may use and credit it, including for commercial release.</li><li>Keep copies of every licence with your delivery documents.</li></ul>"],
  ['Funding, pitching and ethics', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Funding sources:</strong> grants, film funds, sponsors, partners, crowdfunding, savings and pre-sales. Study each funder’s rules and do not mislead anyone about your project or budget.</li><li><strong>A pitch</strong> usually includes: logline, synopsis, why now, why you, audience, look and tone, budget, schedule, team and how funds will be used.</li><li><strong>Ethics:</strong> be honest, pay people fairly and on time, respect consent, protect the vulnerable, credit everyone fairly, avoid stereotypes and represent communities with dignity.</li></ul>"],
 ],
